TEXT 160 -16 Left 3 ;#10 Half-wave rectifier supply, seen DC coupled and AC coupled
TEXT 160 48 Left 2 ;Source: Alan Wolke (W2AEW), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Hkq-fvb5-NI
TEXT 160 96 Left 2 ;One diode and a smoothing capacitor make a rough DC supply. DC coupling shows the whole
TEXT 160 128 Left 2 ;supply voltage; AC coupling shows only the ripple riding on it.
TEXT 160 496 Left 2 !.tran 0 1 990m 1u
TEXT 160 536 Left 2 !.meas tran SUPPLY_AVG AVG V(supply) FROM 995m TO 1
TEXT 160 576 Left 2 !.meas tran SUPPLY_MAX MAX V(supply) FROM 995m TO 1
TEXT 160 616 Left 2 !.meas tran SUPPLY_MIN MIN V(supply) FROM 995m TO 1
TEXT 160 656 Left 2 !.meas tran RIPPLE_PP PP V(supply) FROM 995m TO 1
TEXT 160 696 Left 2 !.meas tran AC_AVG AVG V(scope_ac) FROM 995m TO 1
TEXT 160 736 Left 2 !.meas tran AC_PP PP V(scope_ac) FROM 995m TO 1
TEXT 160 800 Left 2 ;Alan draws this circuit without a single value, so the 10 V peak at 1 kHz, the 1N4148, the 1 k
TEXT 160 832 Left 2 ;load and the 10 uF smoothing capacitor are all choices made here.
TEXT 160 880 Left 2 ;A scope couples through about 1 uF. 100 nF is used here so the 1 MEG input settles inside the
TEXT 160 912 Left 2 ;run; at 1 kHz it still passes the ripple unchanged.
